tion of the heavier oxygen-18 than does seawater.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about water vapor."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The vapor was evaporated from the ocean."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It contains a greater proportion of oxygen-16."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It contains a smaller proportion of oxygen-18."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Oxygen-18 is the heavier of the two."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with seawater."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Normally, this phenomenon has no effect on the overall composition of the ocean, because evaporated seawater returns to the ocean through precipitation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This describes what happens normally."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The phenomenon has no effect."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is unaffected is the ocean''s overall composition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A reason is given for that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Evaporated seawater returns to the ocean."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It returns through precipitation."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "During an ice age, however, a large amount of precipitation falls on ice caps, where it is trapped as ice.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the normal case."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It describes what happens during an ice age."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Precipitation falls on ice caps."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The amount is large."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The precipitation is trapped there."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is trapped as ice."}]}], "the_read": "Ice caps formed during an ice age will have a greater proportion of oxygen-16 and a smaller proportion of oxygen-18.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q11", "prompt": "Some of the most prosperous nations in the world have experienced a pronounced drop in national savings rates—the percentage of after-tax income an average household saves. This trend will undoubtedly continue if the average age of these nations’ populations continues to rise, since older people have fewer reasons to save than do younger people.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about nations. · The nations are among the most prosperous in the world. · It applies to some of them, not all. · They have experienced a drop. · The drop is pronounced. · What dropped is national savings rates. · That rate is a percentage of after-tax income. · It is what an average household saves. (2) This is the conclusion. · It predicts the trend will continue. · The prediction is stated with certainty. · It depends on a condition. · The condition is the average age continuing to rise. · The age is that of these nations'' populations. · A reason is given for the prediction. · Older people have fewer reasons to save than younger people.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some of the most prosperous nations in the world have experienced a pronounced drop in national savings rates—the percentage of after-tax income an average household saves.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about nations."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The nations are among the most prosperous in the world."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It applies to some of them, not all."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have experienced a drop."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The drop is pronounced."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What dropped is national savings rates."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That rate is a percentage of after-tax income."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It is what an average household saves."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This trend will undoubtedly continue if the average age of these nations’ populations continues to rise, since older people have fewer reasons to save than do younger people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It predicts the trend will continue."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The prediction is stated with certainty."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It depends on a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is the average age continuing to rise."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The age is that of these nations'' populations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "A reason is given for the prediction."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Older people have fewer reasons to save than younger people."}]}], "the_read": "What if older people have much better reasons to save, even if they have fewer?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q12", "prompt": "The term “pit bull” does not designate a breed of dog, as do the terms “German shepherd” and “poodle.” It is like the terms “Seeing-Eye dog” and “police dog,” which designate dogs according to what they do. If you take two German shepherds and place them side by side, you cannot tell by appearance alone which is the police dog and which is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the term pit bull. · That term does not designate a breed. · A contrast is drawn with other terms. · German shepherd is one of those terms. · Poodle is the other. · Those terms do designate breeds. (2) A comparison is being made. · The comparison is with two other terms. · One term is Seeing-Eye dog. · The other is police dog. · Those terms designate dogs by what they do. (3) This sets up a situation. · The situation involves two German shepherds. · They are placed side by side. · You cannot tell which is which. · Appearance alone is not enough. · One is the police dog. · The other is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The term “pit bull” does not designate a breed of dog, as do the terms “German shepherd” and “poodle.”", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the term pit bull."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That term does not designate a breed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A contrast is drawn with other terms."}, {"n": 4, "text": "German shepherd is one of those terms."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Poodle is the other."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those terms do designate breeds."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is like the terms “Seeing-Eye dog” and “police dog,” which designate dogs according to what they do.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A comparison is being made."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The comparison is with two other terms."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One term is Seeing-Eye dog."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is police dog."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those terms designate dogs by what they do."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "If you take two German shepherds and place them side by side, you cannot tell by appearance alone which is the police dog and which is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This sets up a situation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The situation involves two German shepherds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are placed side by side."}, {"n": 4, "text": "You cannot tell which is which."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Appearance alone is not enough."}, {"n": 6, "text": "One is the police dog."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The other is the Seeing-Eye dog."}]}], "the_read": "What if a dog''s breed can be determined by what it does? What if dog breed has nothing to do with appearance?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q13", "prompt": "Historically, monetary systems have developed only in population centers with marketplaces. Through the fourth century B.C., Mesopotamian cities engaged in trade, but had never had marketplaces. By that period, however, Greek cities all had marketplaces, or agorae. The Greek cities’ agorae were centrally located and goods were traded there either for money or for commodities.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a historical claim. · It is about monetary systems. · Those systems have developed in only one kind of place. · The places are population centers. · Those centers have marketplaces. (2) This covers a period of time. · The period runs through the fourth century B.C. · It is about Mesopotamian cities. · Those cities engaged in trade. · A contrast follows that. · They had never had marketplaces. (3) This is a contrast with the Mesopotamian cities. · It is about Greek cities. · It refers to the same period. · All of those cities had marketplaces. · The marketplaces were called agorae. (4) This is about the Greek agorae. · They were centrally located. · Goods were traded there. · There were two things goods were traded for. · One of them was money. · The other was commodities.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Historically, monetary systems have developed only in population centers with marketplaces.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a historical claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about monetary systems."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those systems have developed in only one kind of place."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The places are population centers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those centers have marketplaces."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Through the fourth century B.C., Mesopotamian cities engaged in trade, but had never had marketplaces.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This covers a period of time."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The period runs through the fourth century B.C."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about Mesopotamian cities."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cities engaged in trade."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A contrast follows that."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They had never had marketplaces."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "By that period, however, Greek cities all had marketplaces, or agorae.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the Mesopotamian cities."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Greek cities."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It refers to the same period."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All of those cities had marketplaces."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The marketplaces were called agorae."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The Greek cities’ agorae were centrally located and goods were traded there either for money or for commodities.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the Greek agorae."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They were centrally located."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Goods were traded there."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There were two things goods were traded for."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One of them was money."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The other was commodities."}]}], "the_read": "Fourth century Mesopotamian cities did not have monetary systems.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q14", "prompt": "Computer operating system software has become increasingly standardized. But when a large business with multiple, linked computer systems uses identical operating system software on all of its computers, a computer vandal who gains access to one computer automatically has access to the data on all the computers. Using a program known as a “virus,” the vandal can then destroy much of the data on all the computers. If such a business introduced minor variations into its operating system software, unauthorized access to all the computers at the same time could be virtually eliminated. Furthermore, variations in operating system software can be created without any loss of computer compatibility to the business. Therefore, it is advisable for businesses to implement such variations.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about computer operating system software. · That software has become standardized. · The standardization is increasing. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It sets up a condition. · The condition involves a large business. · The business has multiple computer systems. · Those systems are linked. · It uses identical operating system software. · The software is on all of its computers. (3) This gives the result of that condition. · It is about a computer vandal. · The vandal gains access to one computer. · That gives access to all the computers. · What is accessed is the data. · The access is granted automatically. (4) This is about a program. · The program is known as a virus. · The vandal uses it. · The vandal can then destroy data. · Much of the data can be destroyed. · The data is on all the computers. (5) This is a conditional statement. · The condition is the business introducing variations. · The variations are minor ones. · They go into its operating system software. · The result concerns unauthorized access. · The access is to all the computers at the same time. · That access could be eliminated. · The elimination is virtual rather than total. (6) This is an additional point. · It is about variations in operating system software. · Those variations can be created. · Nothing is lost by creating them. · What is not lost is computer compatibility. · The compatibility is the business''s own. (7) This is the conclusion. · It is about businesses. · Something is advisable for them. · What is advisable is implementing such variations.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Computer operating system software has become increasingly standardized.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about computer operating system software."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That software has become standardized."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The standardization is increasing."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But when a large business with multiple, linked computer systems uses identical operating system software on all of its computers,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It sets up a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition involves a large business."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The business has multiple computer systems."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those systems are linked."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It uses identical operating system software."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The software is on all of its computers."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "a computer vandal who gains access to one computer automatically has access to the data on all the computers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the result of that condition."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a computer vandal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The vandal gains access to one computer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That gives access to all the computers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is accessed is the data."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The access is granted automatically."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Using a program known as a “virus,” the vandal can then destroy much of the data on all the computers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a program."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The program is known as a virus."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The vandal uses it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The vandal can then destroy data."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Much of the data can be destroyed."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The data is on all the computers."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "If such a business introduced minor variations into its operating system software, unauthorized access to all the computers at the same time could be virtually eliminated.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The condition is the business introducing variations."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The variations are minor ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They go into its operating system software."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The result concerns unauthorized access."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The access is to all the computers at the same time."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That access could be eliminated."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The elimination is virtual rather than total."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "Furthermore, variations in operating system software can be created without any loss of computer compatibility to the business.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about variations in operating system software."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those variations can be created."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nothing is lost by creating them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is not lost is computer compatibility."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The compatibility is the business''s own."}]}, {"n": 7, "text": "Therefore, it is advisable for businesses to implement such variations.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about businesses."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something is advisable for them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is advisable is implementing such variations."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s a huge downside to variations in operating system software, like it''s very expensive to purchase and maintain, and it''s hard for employees to use?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT1.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q16", "prompt": "It is the mark of a superior conductor that he or she has the authority to insist, even with a top orchestra, that rehearsal work must be intensified. This authority cannot simply be claimed; the conductor must earn it by winning the orchestra’s respect for the artistic interpretations he or she is currently pursuing.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This describes the mark of something. · It is about a superior conductor. · The conductor has authority. · The authority is to insist on something. · What is insisted on is intensified rehearsal work. · This holds even with a top orchestra. (2) This is about that authority. · The authority cannot simply be claimed. · The conductor must earn it instead. · It is earned by winning respect. · The respect comes from the orchestra. · The respect is for artistic interpretations. · Those are the ones the conductor is currently pursuing.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "It is the mark of a superior conductor that he or she has the authority to insist, even with a top orchestra, that rehearsal work must be intensified.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This describes the mark of something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a superior conductor."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conductor has authority."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The authority is to insist on something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is insisted on is intensified rehearsal work."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This holds even with a top orchestra."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This authority cannot simply be claimed; the conductor must earn it by winning the orchestra’s respect for the artistic interpretations he or she is currently pursuing.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about that authority."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The authority cannot simply be claimed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conductor must earn it instead."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is earned by winning respect."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The respect comes from the orchestra."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The respect is for artistic interpretations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those are the ones the conductor is currently pursuing."}]}], "the_read": "What if the orchestra pays zero attention to the conductor and cannot/will not recognize any conductor''s interpretation whatsoever? What if respecting a conductor''s artistic decisions will not sway the orchestra to work harder?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q17", "prompt": "In the United States proven oil reserves—the amount of oil considered extractable from known fields—are at the same level as they were ten years ago. Yet over this same period no new oil fields of any consequence have been discovered, and the annual consumption of domestically produced oil has increased.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the United States. · It is about proven oil reserves. · Proven reserves means oil considered extractable. · The oil comes from known fields. · The reserves are at the same level. · The comparison is with ten years ago. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It covers the same period. · No new oil fields have been discovered. · None of any consequence, at least. · A second point is added. · Annual consumption has increased. · The oil consumed is domestically produced.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In the United States proven oil reserves—the amount of oil considered extractable from known fields—are at the same level as they were ten years ago.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the United States."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about proven oil reserves."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Proven reserves means oil considered extractable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The oil comes from known fields."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The reserves are at the same level."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with ten years ago."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yet over this same period no new oil fields of any consequence have been discovered, and the annual consumption of domestically produced oil has increased.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It covers the same period."}, {"n": 3, "text": "No new oil fields have been discovered."}, {"n": 4, "text": "None of any consequence, at least."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A second point is added."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Annual consumption has increased."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The oil consumed is domestically produced."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can extract more oil from curent fields than previously thought?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q18", "prompt": "Train service suffers when a railroad combines commuter and freight service. By dividing its attention between its freight and commuter customers, a railroad serves neither particularly well. Therefore, if a railroad is going to be a successful business, then it must concentrate exclusively on one of these two markets.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about train service. · That service suffers under a condition. · The condition is a railroad combining two services. · One is commuter service. · The other is freight service. (2) This explains why that happens. · The railroad divides its attention. · The attention is split between two sets of customers. · Those are freight and commuter customers. · The railroad serves neither well. · Neither is served particularly well. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is a conditional statement. · The condition is being a successful business. · The railroad must concentrate on one market. · The concentration must be exclusive. · There are two markets to choose between.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Train service suffers when a railroad combines commuter and freight service.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about train service."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That service suffers under a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is a railroad combining two services."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One is commuter service."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other is freight service."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "By dividing its attention between its freight and commuter customers, a railroad serves neither particularly well.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This explains why that happens."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The railroad divides its attention."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The attention is split between two sets of customers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those are freight and commuter customers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The railroad serves neither well."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Neither is served particularly well."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, if a railroad is going to be a successful business, then it must concentrate exclusively on one of these two markets.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is being a successful business."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The railroad must concentrate on one market."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The concentration must be exclusive."}, {"n": 6, "text": "There are two markets to choose between."}]}], "the_read": "What if a railroad can serve its customers poorly but still be a successful business?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q19", "prompt": "Most people in the United States view neither big nor small business as particularly efficient or dynamic and regard both as providing consumers with fairly priced goods and services. However, most people consistently perceive small business as a force for good in society, whereas big business is perceived as socially responsible only in times of prosperity.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about most people. · Those people are in the United States. · They view neither big nor small business as efficient. · They do not view either as dynamic. · Neither is seen as particularly so. · They regard both as providing fairly priced goods. · Services are included as well. · What is provided goes to consumers. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about most people again. · They perceive small business as a force for good. · That perception is consistent. · The good is in society. · Big business is perceived differently. · It is seen as socially responsible only sometimes. · That is in times of prosperity.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Most people in the United States view neither big nor small business as particularly efficient or dynamic and regard both as providing consumers with fairly priced goods and services.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about most people."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those people are in the United States."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They view neither big nor small business as efficient."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They do not view either as dynamic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Neither is seen as particularly so."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They regard both as providing fairly priced goods."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Services are included as well."}, {"n": 8, "text": "What is provided goes to consumers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, most people consistently perceive small business as a force for good in society, whereas big business is perceived as socially responsible only in times of prosperity.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about most people again."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They perceive small business as a force for good."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That perception is consistent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The good is in society."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Big business is perceived differently."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It is seen as socially responsible only sometimes."}, {"n": 8, "text": "That is in times of prosperity."}]}], "the_read": "Businesses can be perceived as a force for good in society even if they are not perceived as being particularly dynamic.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q20", "prompt": "The energy an animal must expend to move uphill is proportional to its body weight, whereas the animal’s energy output available to perform this task is proportional to its surface area. This is the reason that small animals, like squirrels, can run up a tree trunk almost as fast as they can move on level ground, whereas large animals tend to slow down when they are moving uphill.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about an animal. · It is about energy the animal must expend. · The energy is for moving uphill. · That energy is proportional to something. · It is proportional to body weight. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about the animal''s energy output. · That output is the amount available. · It is available to perform this task. · It is proportional to surface area. (3) This states a reason for what follows. · It is about small animals. · Squirrels are given as the example. · They can run up a tree trunk. · They do it almost as fast as on level ground. · The comparison is with level ground. (4) This is a contrast with the small animals. · It is about large animals. · They tend to slow down. · This is a tendency rather than a rule. · It happens when they are moving uphill.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The energy an animal must expend to move uphill is proportional to its body weight,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about an animal."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about energy the animal must expend."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The energy is for moving uphill."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That energy is proportional to something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is proportional to body weight."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "whereas the animal’s energy output available to perform this task is proportional to its surface area.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the animal''s energy output."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That output is the amount available."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is available to perform this task."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is proportional to surface area."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the reason that small animals, like squirrels, can run up a tree trunk almost as fast as they can move on level ground,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This states a reason for what follows."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about small animals."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Squirrels are given as the example."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They can run up a tree trunk."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They do it almost as fast as on level ground."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with level ground."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "whereas large animals tend to slow down when they are moving uphill.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the small animals."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about large animals."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They tend to slow down."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a tendency rather than a rule."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It happens when they are moving uphill."}]}], "the_read": "What if small animals have a much smaller surface area to body weight ratio than large animals?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q21", "prompt": "The 1980s have been characterized as a period of selfish individualism that threatens the cohesion of society. But this characterization is true of any time. Throughout history all human actions have been motivated by selfishness. When the deeper implications are considered, even the simplest “unselfish” acts prove to be instances of selfish concern for the human species.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the 1980s. · That decade has been characterized in a certain way. · It is called a period of selfish individualism. · That individualism threatens something. · What is threatened is the cohesion of society. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about that characterization. · The characterization is true. · It is true of any time, not just the 1980s. (3) This covers the whole of history. · It is about human actions. · It applies to all of them. · They have been motivated by selfishness. (4) This depends on considering deeper implications. · It is about unselfish acts. · It applies even to the simplest of them. · Those acts prove to be something else. · They are instances of selfish concern. · The concern is for the human species.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The 1980s have been characterized as a period of selfish individualism that threatens the cohesion of society.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the 1980s."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That decade has been characterized in a certain way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is called a period of selfish individualism."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That individualism threatens something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is threatened is the cohesion of society."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But this characterization is true of any time.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about that characterization."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The characterization is true."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is true of any time, not just the 1980s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Throughout history all human actions have been motivated by selfishness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This covers the whole of history."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about human actions."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It applies to all of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have been motivated by selfishness."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "When the deeper implications are considered, even the simplest “unselfish” acts prove to be instances of selfish concern for the human species.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This depends on considering deeper implications."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about unselfish acts."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It applies even to the simplest of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those acts prove to be something else."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are instances of selfish concern."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The concern is for the human species."}]}], "the_read": "What if selfish individualism is different from selfish concern for human species?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q22", "prompt": "A medical journal used a questionnaire survey to determine whether a particular change in its format would increase its readership. Sixty-two percent of those who returned the questionnaire supported that change. On the basis of this outcome, the decision was made to introduce the new format.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a medical journal. · The journal used a survey. · The survey was a questionnaire. · It was used to determine something. · The question was about a change in format. · The change is a particular one. · The issue is whether it would increase readership. (2) This gives a figure. · The figure is sixty-two percent. · It is a percentage of a specific group. · The group is those who returned the questionnaire. · Those people supported the change. (3) This rests on that outcome. · A decision was made. · The decision was to introduce the new format.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A medical journal used a questionnaire survey to determine whether a particular change in its format would increase its readership.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a medical journal."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The journal used a survey."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The survey was a questionnaire."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It was used to determine something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The question was about a change in format."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The change is a particular one."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The issue is whether it would increase readership."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Sixty-two percent of those who returned the questionnaire supported that change.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a figure."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The figure is sixty-two percent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is a percentage of a specific group."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The group is those who returned the questionnaire."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those people supported the change."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "On the basis of this outcome, the decision was made to introduce the new format.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This rests on that outcome."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A decision was made."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The decision was to introduce the new format."}]}], "the_read": "What if the sample size of those who returned the survey is too small? What if the people who are in favor of the change are disproportionately sampled among those who returned the survey?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q23", "prompt": "Shanna: Owners of any work of art, simply by virtue of ownership, ethically have the right to destroy that artwork if they find it morally or aesthetically distasteful, or if caring for it becomes inconvenient.\n\nJorge: Ownership of unique artworks, unlike ownership of other kinds of objects, carries the moral right to possess but not to destroy. A unique work of art with aesthetic or historical value belongs to posterity and so must be preserved, whatever the personal wishes of its legal owner.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The first speaker is named Shanna. · She is talking about owners. · The owners are of any work of art. · Their claim rests on ownership alone. (2) The owners have a right. · The right is an ethical one. · The right is to destroy the artwork. · It applies under certain conditions. · One is finding it morally distasteful. · Another is finding it aesthetically distasteful. · Another is caring for it becoming inconvenient. (3) The second speaker is named Jorge. · He is talking about ownership of unique artworks. · He contrasts that with other kinds of objects. · That ownership carries a moral right. · The right is to possess the artwork. · The right does not extend to destroying it. (4) This is about a unique work of art. · The work has aesthetic or historical value. · It belongs to posterity. · It must therefore be preserved. · That holds whatever the owner wishes. · The owner in question is the legal owner.", "category": "Counter", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Shanna: Owners of any work of art, simply by virtue of ownership,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The first speaker is named Shanna."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She is talking about owners."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The owners are of any work of art."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Their claim rests on ownership alone."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "ethically have the right to destroy that artwork if they find it morally or aesthetically distasteful, or if caring for it becomes inconvenient.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The owners have a right."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The right is an ethical one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The right is to destroy the artwork."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It applies under certain conditions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One is finding it morally distasteful."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Another is finding it aesthetically distasteful."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Another is caring for it becoming inconvenient."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Jorge: Ownership of unique artworks, unlike ownership of other kinds of objects, carries the moral right to possess but not to destroy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The second speaker is named Jorge."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He is talking about ownership of unique artworks."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He contrasts that with other kinds of objects."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That ownership carries a moral right."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The right is to possess the artwork."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The right does not extend to destroying it."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "A unique work of art with aesthetic or historical value belongs to posterity and so must be preserved, whatever the personal wishes of its legal owner.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a unique work of art."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The work has aesthetic or historical value."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It belongs to posterity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It must therefore be preserved."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That holds whatever the owner wishes."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The owner in question is the legal owner."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether art owners have the right to destroy any of their artworks.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Counter", "correct_answer": "E"}, {"citation": "PT1.S3.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "A"}]}]'::jsonb,
