    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read it, say it back, react to it. Then the stimulus disappears and you say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Work through the stimulus out loud, a piece at a time. When you have got all of it, press space. The text goes away, and you say the entire argument back from memory.", "rules": ["Everything here is out loud. The whole section records in one take, and it never pauses.", "Read silently, about a sentence at a time. When you are not sure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away and say it back in your own words. Then react to it. Both out loud.", "Keep going like that through the whole stimulus. You pick the pieces. Nothing on screen is counting them.", "Press space when you have got all of it. The stimulus vanishes and stays gone. Now say the whole argument back from memory, with every detail you can hold.", "Press space again and the next one loads. No breaks, no going back, all the way to the end."], "briefing": {"lede": "Worth reading properly once. After that, skim the rules above and go.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still arrive with nothing.", "body": ["You follow a stimulus, sentence by sentence, and it all makes sense as it goes. Then you get down to answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and it is the one this drill was built for.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. If the argument is not still with you while you work the answers, your understanding never got the chance to help.", "Most of what you call reading is built for getting to the end. Scanning names. Dropping the connecting words. Turning a list of three into one label. That is a fine way to read a phone bill. It is a bad way to read something that is about to ask you for every detail back."]}, {"heading": "What you actually do.", "body": ["Three beats through the stimulus, then a fourth once you have got all of it. You decide how much to take on at a time, and nothing on the screen is tracking where you are.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it.", "body": "Intake only. Nothing comes back out yet. Take on about a sentence, with full intonation so every word gets its weight. When you are unsure how much to take, take less. What gets lost when you grab too much is always the front of it."}, {"label": "Say it back.", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, out loud, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it.", "body": "React. A doubt, a question, an example, a joke, a feeling. One or two seconds is plenty. It does not have to be clever, it has to be yours."}, {"label": "The Recall.", "body": "Press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Now say the entire argument back from memory, out loud, with every detail you can still hold. This is the part that gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter.", "body": ["Do not give back the gist, or the summary, or the important parts. You cannot know which parts were important until you have read the whole thing, so filtering on the way through mostly means quietly throwing out the piece you were going to need.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. The names, the numbers, and the small words carrying the load, all, most, only, probably, they either come back or they do not.", "**Efficiency is never a licence to leave information out.**"]}, {"heading": "You will think you have nothing to say. Everybody does.", "body": ["You will open your mouth to colour something and find nothing there. That lasts about a week.", "It is not that you have no thoughts. It is that nobody has ever asked you for one in the middle of reading before, and the habit takes a few sessions to wake up. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count.", "The reaction is what makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away. That part is on you.", "body": ["While you are working the stimulus, nothing on the screen hides. The whole thing stays readable, which means the only thing stopping you rereading a line while you say it back is you, looking away from it.", "That is deliberate. Reading while you recall is a one way street with cars coming both ways. It jams. You stop using your memory at all and slide straight back into the author''s exact words instead of finding your own.", "It is also the version that still works on paper, and on test day. A screen that hides the text for you is a crutch that cannot come with you."]}, {"heading": "Twenty-five minutes, and what that number means.", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section, and it only counts if every stimulus got recalled, coloured, and given a Read. Finishing early with four of them skipped is not finishing early. If you have extra time on test day, your goal scales with it.", "The clock in the corner counts up, not down. It is there so you can pace a section, and it is deliberately not counting towards zero, because a number falling away in the corner of your eye produces exactly the skating read this drill exists to fix.", "It is not a race. It is the pace at which this stops being a study exercise and starts being how you read under a clock. Go slower while it is new. The speed shows up on its own once the method does."]}], "closer": "One a day holds you steady. The second one is where you improve."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, says it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives a Read of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Read against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q1", "prompt": "Everyone sitting in the waiting room of the school’s athletic office this morning at nine o’clock had just registered for a beginners tennis clinic. John, Mary, and Teresa were all sitting in the waiting room this morning at nine o’clock. No accomplished tennis player would register for a beginners tennis clinic.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about everyone in a waiting room. · The waiting room belongs to the school''s athletic office. · The time was this morning at nine o''clock. · All of those people had just registered for something. · What they registered for is a tennis clinic. · The clinic is for beginners. (2) This is about three named people. · One of them is John. · One of them is Mary. · One of them is Teresa. · All three were in the waiting room. · The time was this morning at nine o''clock. (3) This is about accomplished tennis players. · It applies to all of them. · None of them would register. · What they would not register for is a beginners tennis clinic.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Everyone sitting in the waiting room of the school’s athletic office this morning at nine o’clock had just registered for a beginners tennis clinic.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about everyone in a waiting room."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The waiting room belongs to the school''s athletic office."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The time was this morning at nine o''clock."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All of those people had just registered for something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What they registered for is a tennis clinic."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The clinic is for beginners."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "John, Mary, and Teresa were all sitting in the waiting room this morning at nine o’clock.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about three named people."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One of them is John."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One of them is Mary."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One of them is Teresa."}, {"n": 5, "text": "All three were in the waiting room."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The time was this morning at nine o''clock."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "No accomplished tennis player would register for a beginners tennis clinic.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about accomplished tennis players."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It applies to all of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "None of them would register."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they would not register for is a beginners tennis clinic."}]}], "the_read": "John, Mary, and Teresa are not accomplished tennis players.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q2", "prompt": "Most people who ride bicycles for pleasure do not ride until the warm weather of spring and summer arrives. Yet it is probably more effective to advertise bicycles earlier in the year. Most bicycles are purchased in the spring, but once shoppers are ready to shop for a bicycle, they usually have already decided which brand and model of bicycle they will purchase. By then it is generally too late to induce them to change their minds.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about people who ride bicycles. · They ride for pleasure. · It is about most of them, not all. · They do not ride until certain weather arrives. · The weather is warm. · That warm weather comes in spring and summer. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about advertising bicycles. · Advertising earlier in the year is more effective. · This is stated as probable rather than certain. (3) Most bicycles are purchased in the spring. · A contrast follows that. · It is about shoppers ready to shop. · Those shoppers have already decided something. · They have decided which brand they will buy. · They have decided which model as well. · This is usually the case rather than always. (4) This is about that point in time. · It is too late by then. · What is too late is inducing them to change their minds. · This is generally so rather than always.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Most people who ride bicycles for pleasure do not ride until the warm weather of spring and summer arrives.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about people who ride bicycles."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They ride for pleasure."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about most of them, not all."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They do not ride until certain weather arrives."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The weather is warm."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That warm weather comes in spring and summer."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yet it is probably more effective to advertise bicycles earlier in the year.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about advertising bicycles."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Advertising earlier in the year is more effective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is stated as probable rather than certain."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most bicycles are purchased in the spring, but once shoppers are ready to shop for a bicycle, they usually have already decided which brand and model of bicycle they will purchase.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Most bicycles are purchased in the spring."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A contrast follows that."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about shoppers ready to shop."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those shoppers have already decided something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They have decided which brand they will buy."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They have decided which model as well."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is usually the case rather than always."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "By then it is generally too late to induce them to change their minds.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about that point in time."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is too late by then."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is too late is inducing them to change their minds."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is generally so rather than always."}]}], "the_read": "What if shoppers never choose which bicycle to purchase based on advertising, so advertising will be ineffective any time of year?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q3", "prompt": "During 1991 the number of people in the town of Bayburg who received municipal food assistance doubled, even though the number of people in Bayburg whose incomes were low enough to qualify for such assistance remained unchanged.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the year 1991. · The place is a town called Bayburg. · It is about people who received food assistance. · The assistance is municipal. · The number of them doubled. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about people in Bayburg. · Those people have low enough incomes to qualify. · What they would qualify for is such assistance. · Their number remained unchanged.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "During 1991 the number of people in the town of Bayburg who received municipal food assistance doubled,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the year 1991."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The place is a town called Bayburg."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about people who received food assistance."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The assistance is municipal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The number of them doubled."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "even though the number of people in Bayburg whose incomes were low enough to qualify for such assistance remained unchanged.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about people in Bayburg."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people have low enough incomes to qualify."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they would qualify for is such assistance."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Their number remained unchanged."}]}], "the_read": "What if there was a public awareness campaign that encouraged those who were eligible, but had never applied for food assistance, to apply?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q4", "prompt": "Campaigning for election to provincial or state office frequently requires that a candidate spend much time and energy catering to the interests of national party officials who can help the candidate to win office. The elected officials who campaign for reelection while they are in office thus often fail to serve the interests of their local constituencies.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about campaigning for election. · The office is provincial or state office. · Campaigning requires something of a candidate. · This is frequently so rather than always. · The candidate spends much time and energy. · It is spent catering to interests. · The interests belong to national party officials. · Those officials can help the candidate win. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about elected officials. · Those officials campaign for reelection. · They do it while they are in office. · They fail to serve certain interests. · The interests belong to their local constituencies. · This happens often rather than always.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Campaigning for election to provincial or state office frequently requires that a candidate spend much time and energy catering to the interests of national party officials who can help the candidate to win office.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about campaigning for election."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The office is provincial or state office."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Campaigning requires something of a candidate."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is frequently so rather than always."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The candidate spends much time and energy."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is spent catering to interests."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The interests belong to national party officials."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Those officials can help the candidate win."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The elected officials who campaign for reelection while they are in office thus often fail to serve the interests of their local constituencies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about elected officials."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those officials campaign for reelection."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They do it while they are in office."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They fail to serve certain interests."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The interests belong to their local constituencies."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This happens often rather than always."}]}], "the_read": "What if local constituent interests are the same as national party interests?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q5", "prompt": "Since Professor Smythe has been head of the department, the most distinguished member of the faculty has resigned, fewer new courses have been developed, student enrollment has dropped, and the reputation of the department has gone down. These facts provide conclusive evidence that Professor Smythe was appointed to undermine the department.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This covers a period of time. · The period is since Professor Smythe became head. · Smythe is head of the department. · A member of the faculty has resigned. · That member was the most distinguished one. (2) This adds more things that happened. · Fewer new courses have been developed. · Student enrollment has dropped. · The department''s reputation has gone down. (3) This is the conclusion. · It rests on those facts. · The facts are called conclusive evidence. · The claim is about Professor Smythe''s appointment. · He was appointed for a purpose. · The purpose was to undermine the department.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Since Professor Smythe has been head of the department, the most distinguished member of the faculty has resigned,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This covers a period of time."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The period is since Professor Smythe became head."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Smythe is head of the department."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A member of the faculty has resigned."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That member was the most distinguished one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "fewer new courses have been developed, student enrollment has dropped, and the reputation of the department has gone down.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds more things that happened."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Fewer new courses have been developed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Student enrollment has dropped."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The department''s reputation has gone down."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "These facts provide conclusive evidence that Professor Smythe was appointed to undermine the department.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on those facts."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The facts are called conclusive evidence."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claim is about Professor Smythe''s appointment."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He was appointed for a purpose."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The purpose was to undermine the department."}]}], "the_read": "What if Professor Smythe''s appointment didn''t cause any of these negative events? What if just because all of these things happened after Smythe''s appointment, that doesn''t mean it was intentional?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q6", "prompt": "Books about architectural works, unless they are not intended for a general audience, ought to include discussions of both the utility and the aesthetic appeal of each of the buildings they consider. If they do not, they are flawed. Morton’s book on Italian Baroque palaces describes these palaces’ functional aspects, but fails to mention that the main hall of a palace he discusses at length has a ceiling that is one of the truly breathtaking masterpieces of Western art.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about books. · The books are about architectural works. · An exception is being set out. · The exception is books not intended for a general audience. (2) Those books ought to include discussions. · There are two things to discuss. · One is the utility of the buildings. · The other is their aesthetic appeal. · This applies to each building the book considers. (3) This is a conditional statement. · The condition is a book not doing that. · Such a book is flawed. (4) This is about a book by Morton. · The book is on Italian Baroque palaces. · The book describes something. · What it describes is the palaces'' functional aspects. (5) This is a contrast with what the book does. · The book fails to mention something. · It is about the main hall of a palace. · Morton discusses that palace at length. · The hall has a ceiling. · The ceiling is one of the masterpieces of Western art. · It is described as truly breathtaking.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Books about architectural works, unless they are not intended for a general audience,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about books."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The books are about architectural works."}, {"n": 3, "text": "An exception is being set out."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The exception is books not intended for a general audience."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "ought to include discussions of both the utility and the aesthetic appeal of each of the buildings they consider.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Those books ought to include discussions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are two things to discuss."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One is the utility of the buildings."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is their aesthetic appeal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This applies to each building the book considers."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "If they do not, they are flawed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The condition is a book not doing that."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a book is flawed."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Morton’s book on Italian Baroque palaces describes these palaces’ functional aspects,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a book by Morton."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The book is on Italian Baroque palaces."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The book describes something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What it describes is the palaces'' functional aspects."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "but fails to mention that the main hall of a palace he discusses at length has a ceiling that is one of the truly breathtaking masterpieces of Western art.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what the book does."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The book fails to mention something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about the main hall of a palace."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Morton discusses that palace at length."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The hall has a ceiling."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The ceiling is one of the masterpieces of Western art."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It is described as truly breathtaking."}]}], "the_read": "If Morton''s book is intended for a general audience, then it is flawed.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q7", "prompt": "Of all the photographs taken of him at his wedding, there was one that John and his friends sharply disagreed about. His friends all said that this particular picture did not much resemble him, but John said that on the contrary it was the only photograph that did.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about photographs. · The photographs were taken of John. · They were taken at his wedding. · One of them was disagreed about. · The disagreement was between John and his friends. · The disagreement was sharp. (2) This is about what his friends said. · All of them said the same thing. · They said the picture did not much resemble him. · A contrast follows with what John said. · John said the opposite. · He said it was the only photograph that resembled him.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Of all the photographs taken of him at his wedding, there was one that John and his friends sharply disagreed about.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about photographs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The photographs were taken of John."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were taken at his wedding."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One of them was disagreed about."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The disagreement was between John and his friends."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The disagreement was sharp."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "His friends all said that this particular picture did not much resemble him, but John said that on the contrary it was the only photograph that did.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about what his friends said."}, {"n": 2, "text": "All of them said the same thing."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They said the picture did not much resemble him."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A contrast follows with what John said."}, {"n": 5, "text": "John said the opposite."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He said it was the only photograph that resembled him."}]}], "the_read": "What if John''s vision of himself is very different from his friends'' vision of him?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q8", "prompt": "Eva: A “smart highway” system should be installed, one that would monitor areawide traffic patterns and communicate with computers in vehicles or with programmable highway signs to give drivers information about traffic congestion and alternate routes. Such a system, we can infer, would result in improved traffic flow in and around cities that would do more than improve drivers’ tempers; it would decrease the considerable loss of money and productivity that now results from traffic congestion.\n\nLuis: There are already traffic reports on the radio. Why would a “smart highway” system be any better?", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The first speaker is named Eva. · She is talking about a system. · The system is called a smart highway. · She says it should be installed. (2) The system would monitor traffic patterns. · The patterns are areawide. · The system would also communicate. · It could communicate with computers in vehicles. · It could instead communicate with programmable highway signs. · The purpose is to give drivers information. · The information is about traffic congestion. · It is also about alternate routes. (3) This is stated as something we can infer. · The system would improve traffic flow. · The improvement is in and around cities. · It would do more than one thing. · Improving drivers'' tempers is not all of it. (4) The system would decrease a loss. · The loss is of money. · The loss is also of productivity. · The loss is considerable. · It results from traffic congestion. · This is happening now. (5) The second speaker is named Luis. · He points out something that already exists. · Traffic reports exist already. · The reports are on the radio. (6) This is a question. · The question is about the smart highway system. · It asks why the system would be better. · It doubts that it would be better at all.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Eva: A “smart highway” system should be installed,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The first speaker is named Eva."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She is talking about a system."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The system is called a smart highway."}, {"n": 4, "text": "She says it should be installed."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "one that would monitor areawide traffic patterns and communicate with computers in vehicles or with programmable highway signs to give drivers information about traffic congestion and alternate routes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The system would monitor traffic patterns."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The patterns are areawide."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The system would also communicate."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It could communicate with computers in vehicles."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It could instead communicate with programmable highway signs."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The purpose is to give drivers information."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The information is about traffic congestion."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It is also about alternate routes."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a system, we can infer, would result in improved traffic flow in and around cities that would do more than improve drivers’ tempers;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is stated as something we can infer."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The system would improve traffic flow."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The improvement is in and around cities."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It would do more than one thing."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Improving drivers'' tempers is not all of it."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "it would decrease the considerable loss of money and productivity that now results from traffic congestion.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The system would decrease a loss."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The loss is of money."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The loss is also of productivity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The loss is considerable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It results from traffic congestion."