his is the more proper use of the term. (3) This contrasts with the term sex. · It is about the term gender. · That term refers to a construction by society. · The construction is a system. · The system identifies what is masculine. · It also identifies what is feminine. (4) A comparison is being drawn. · One side is the characteristics defining biological sex. · The other side is the traits associated with gender. · The gender traits do not sort people into two groups. · Those groups would have to be nonoverlapping. (5) The traits characterize people. · They do it in a complex way. · A result follows from that. · A person may have masculine traits. · The same person may have feminine traits. · Having both is possible.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The terms “sex” and “gender” are often used interchangeably.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about two terms."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One term is sex."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other term is gender."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are used interchangeably."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This happens often rather than always."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But “sex” more properly refers to biological differences of male and female,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the term sex."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That term refers to biological differences."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The differences are of male and female."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is the more proper use of the term."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "while “gender” refers to society’s construction of a system that identifies what is masculine and feminine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This contrasts with the term sex."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the term gender."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That term refers to a construction by society."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The construction is a system."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The system identifies what is masculine."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It also identifies what is feminine."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Unlike the set of characteristics defining biological sex, the set of traits that are associated with gender does not sort people into two nonoverlapping groups.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A comparison is being drawn."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One side is the characteristics defining biological sex."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other side is the traits associated with gender."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The gender traits do not sort people into two groups."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those groups would have to be nonoverlapping."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The traits characterize people in a complex way, so that a person may have both “masculine” and “feminine” traits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The traits characterize people."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They do it in a complex way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A result follows from that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A person may have masculine traits."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The same person may have feminine traits."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Having both is possible."}]}], "the_read": "Someone of male sex does not need to have exclusively masculine gender traits.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q11", "prompt": "Raising the tax rate on essential goods—a traditional means of increasing government revenues—invariably turns low- and middle-income taxpayers against the government. Hence government officials have proposed adding a new tax on purchases of luxury items such as yachts, private planes, jewels, and furs. The officials claim that this tax will result in a substantial increase in government revenues while affecting only the wealthy individuals and corporations who can afford to purchase such items.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about raising a tax rate. · The tax is on essential goods. · Doing this is a traditional 