ny sweetness receptor.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the present."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The researchers claim a discovery."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they discovered is a substance."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Only one molecule of it is needed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One molecule can activate any sweetness receptor."}]}], "the_read": "The newly discovered substance will be perceived to be very sweet.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q17", "prompt": "An editorial in the _Grandburg Daily Herald_ claims that Grandburg’s voters would generally welcome the defeat of the political party now in control of the Grandburg City Council. The editorial bases its claim on a recent survey that found that 59 percent of Grandburg’s registered voters think that the party will definitely be out of power after next year’s city council elections.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about an editorial. · The editorial is in the Grandburg Daily Herald. · It makes a claim about Grandburg''s voters. · Those voters would welcome something. · They would generally welcome it rather than all of them. · What they would welcome is the defeat of a political party. · That party is now in control. · It controls the Grandburg City Council. (2) This is the basis for the editorial''s claim. · The basis is a survey. · The survey is a recent one. · It found a figure of 59 percent. · The percentage is of registered voters in Grandburg. · Those voters think something. · They think the party will be out of power. · It would happen after next year''s city council elections.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "An editorial in the _Grandburg Daily Herald_ claims that Grandburg’s voters would generally welcome the defeat of the political party now in control of the Grandburg City Council.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The editorial is in the Grandburg Daily Herald."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It makes a claim about Grandburg''s voters."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those voters would welcome something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They would generally welcome it rather than all of them."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What they would welcome is the defeat of a political party."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That party is now in control."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It controls the Grandburg City Council."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The editorial bases its claim on a recent survey that found that 59 percent of Grandburg’s registered voters think that the party will definitely be out of power after next year’s city council elections.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the basis for the editorial''s claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The basis is a survey."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The survey is a recent one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It found a figure of 59 percent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The percentage is of registered voters in Grandburg."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those voters think something."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They think the party will be out of power."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It would happen after next year''s city council elections."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because a majority expects something to happen, that doens''t mean a majority would be happy about that thing happening?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q18", "prompt": "Prolonged exposure to nonionizing radiation— electromagnetic radiation at or below the frequency of visible light—increases a person’s chances of developing soft-tissue cancer. Electric power lines as well as such electrical appliances as electric blankets and video-display terminals are sources of nonionizing radiation.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanati