  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt105-s2',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT105 Section 2',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read it, say it back, react to it. Then the stimulus disappears and you say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Work through the stimulus out loud, a piece at a time. When you have got all of it, press space. The text goes away, and you say the entire argument back from memory.", "rules": ["Everything here is out loud. The whole section records in one take, and it never pauses.", "Read silently, about a sentence at a time. When you are not sure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away and say it back in your own words. Then react to it. Both out loud.", "Keep going like that through the whole stimulus. You pick the pieces. Nothing on screen is counting them.", "Press space when you have got all of it. The stimulus vanishes and stays gone. Now say the whole argument back from memory, with every detail you can hold.", "Press space again and the next one loads. No breaks, no going back, all the way to the end."], "briefing": {"lede": "Worth reading properly once. After that, skim the rules above and go.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still arrive with nothing.", "body": ["You follow a stimulus, sentence by sentence, and it all makes sense as it goes. Then you get down to answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and it is the one this drill was built for.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. If the argument is not still with you while you work the answers, your understanding never got the chance to help.", "Most of what you call reading is built for getting to the end. Scanning names. Dropping the connecting words. Turning a list of three into one label. That is a fine way to read a phone bill. It is a bad way to read something that is about to ask you for every detail back."]}, {"heading": "What you actually do.", "body": ["Three beats through the stimulus, then a fourth once you have got all of it. You decide how much to take on at a time, and nothing on the screen is tracking where you are.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it.", "body": "Intake only. Nothing comes back out yet. Take on about a sentence, with full intonation so every word gets its weight. When you are unsure how much to take, take less. What gets lost when you grab too much is always the front of it."}, {"label": "Say it back.", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, out loud, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it.", "body": "React. A doubt, a question, an example, a joke, a feeling. One or two seconds is plenty. It does not have to be clever, it has to be yours."}, {"label": "The Recall.", "body": "Press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Now say the entire argument back from memory, out loud, with every detail you can still hold. This is the part that gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter.", "body": ["Do not give back the gist, or the summary, or the important parts. You cannot know which parts were important until you have read the whole thing, so filtering on the way through mostly means quietly throwing out the piece you were going to need.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. The names, the numbers, and the small words carrying the load, all, most, only, probably, they either come back or they do not.", "**Efficiency is never a licence to leave information out.**"]}, {"heading": "You will think you have nothing to say. Everybody does.", "body": ["You will open your mouth to colour something and find nothing there. That lasts about a week.", "It is not that you have no thoughts. It is that nobody has ever asked you for one in the middle of reading before, and the habit takes a few sessions to wake up. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count.", "The reaction is what makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away. That part is on you.", "body": ["While you are working the stimulus, nothing on the screen hides. The whole thing stays readable, which means the only thing stopping you rereading a line while you say it back is you, looking away from it.", "That is deliberate. Reading while you recall is a one way street with cars coming both ways. It jams. You stop using your memory at all and slide straight back into the author''s exact words instead of finding your own.", "It is also the version that still works on paper, and on test day. A screen that hides the text for you is a crutch that cannot come with you."]}, {"heading": "Twenty-five minutes, and what that number means.", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section, and it only counts if every stimulus got recalled, coloured, and given a Read. Finishing early with four of them skipped is not finishing early. If you have extra time on test day, your goal scales with it.", "The clock in the corner counts up, not down. It is there so you can pace a section, and it is deliberately not counting towards zero, because a number falling away in the corner of your eye produces exactly the skating read this drill exists to fix.", "It is not a race. It is the pace at which this stops being a study exercise and starts being how you read under a clock. Go slower while it is new. The speed shows up on its own once the method does."]}], "closer": "One a day holds you steady. The second one is where you improve."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, says it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives a Read of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Read against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q1", "prompt": "French divers recently found a large cave along the coast of the Mediterranean Sea. The cave is accessible only through an underwater tunnel. The interior of the cave is completely filled with seawater and contains numerous large stalagmites, which are stony pillars that form when drops of water fall repeatedly on a single spot on a cave floor, leaving behind mineral deposits that accumulate over time.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Divers found a cave. · The divers were French. · The find was recent. · The cave is large. · The cave is along a coast. · The coast is the Mediterranean Sea''s. (2) There is only one way into the cave. · The way in is a tunnel. · The tunnel is underwater. (3) This is about the inside of the cave. · It is filled with water. · The water is seawater. · It is filled completely. · The cave contains stalagmites. · The stalagmites are numerous. · The stalagmites are large. (4) Stalagmites are a kind of pillar. · The pillars are made of stone. · They form from falling drops of water. · The drops fall repeatedly. · The drops fall on one single spot. · The spot is on a cave floor. · The drops leave mineral deposits behind. · The deposits build up over time.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "French divers recently found a large cave along the coast of the Mediterranean Sea.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Divers found a cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The divers were French."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The find was recent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cave is large."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cave is along a coast."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The coast is the Mediterranean Sea''s."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The cave is accessible only through an underwater tunnel.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is only one way into the cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The way in is a tunnel."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The tunnel is underwater."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The interior of the cave is completely filled with seawater and contains numerous large stalagmites,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the inside of the cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is filled with water."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The water is seawater."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is filled completely."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cave contains stalagmites."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The stalagmites are numerous."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The stalagmites are large."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "which are stony pillars that form when drops of water fall repeatedly on a single spot on a cave floor, leaving behind mineral deposits that accumulate over time.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Stalagmites are a kind of pillar."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The pillars are made of stone."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They form from falling drops of water."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The drops fall repeatedly."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The drops fall on one single spot."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The spot is on a cave floor."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The drops leave mineral deposits behind."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The deposits build up over time."}]}], "the_read": "The cave was not always completely filled with water.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q2", "prompt": "A director of the Rexx Pharmaceutical Company argued that the development costs for new vaccines that the health department has requested should be subsidized by the government, since the marketing of vaccines promised to be less profitable than the marketing of any other pharmaceutical product. In support of this claim the director argued that sales of vaccines are likely to be lower since each vaccine is administered to a patient only once, whereas medicines that combat diseases and chronic illnesses are administered many times to each patient.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A director made an argument. · The director is at Rexx Pharmaceutical Company. · The argument is about development costs. · The costs are for new vaccines. · The health department requested those vaccines. · He wants the costs subsidized. · The government should do the subsidizing. (2) This is his reason. · It is about marketing vaccines. · Marketing vaccines is expected to be less profitable. · The comparison is with marketing any other pharmaceutical product. (3) This is further support for the claim. · Vaccine sales are likely to be lower. · The reason is how often a vaccine is given. · A vaccine is given to a patient only once. (4) This is the contrast. · It is about other medicines. · Those medicines fight diseases and chronic illnesses. · Those are given many times to each patient.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A director of the Rexx Pharmaceutical Company argued that the development costs for new vaccines that the health department has requested should be subsidized by the government,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A director made an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The director is at Rexx Pharmaceutical Company."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The argument is about development costs."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The costs are for new vaccines."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The health department requested those vaccines."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He wants the costs subsidized."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The government should do the subsidizing."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "since the marketing of vaccines promised to be less profitable than the marketing of any other pharmaceutical product.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about marketing vaccines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Marketing vaccines is expected to be less profitable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with marketing any other pharmaceutical product."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In support of this claim the director argued that sales of vaccines are likely to be lower since each vaccine is administered to a patient only once,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is further support for the claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Vaccine sales are likely to be lower."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is how often a vaccine is given."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A vaccine is given to a patient only once."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "whereas medicines that combat diseases and chronic illnesses are administered many times to each patient.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about other medicines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those medicines fight diseases and chronic illnesses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those are given many times to each patient."}]}], "the_read": "What if it''s obligatory for pharmaceutical companies to absorb the costs of developing vaccines as a contribution to the public good? What if the government is not obliged to subsidized less profitable pharmaceutical products?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q3", "prompt": "Manager: Our new computer network, the purpose of which is to increase productivity, can be installed during the day, which would disrupt our employees'' work, or else at night, which would entail much higher installation charges. Since saving money is important, we should have the network installed during the day.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A manager is speaking. · There is a new computer network. · Its purpose is to increase productivity. · It can be installed during the day. (2) That option would disrupt work. · The work disrupted is the employees''. · The other option is installing at night. · Night installation would cost much more. (3) Saving money is important. · That is the reason given. · The conclusion is to install during the day.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Manager: Our new computer network, the purpose of which is to increase productivity, can be installed during the day,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A manager is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a new computer network."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Its purpose is to increase productivity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It can be installed during the day."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "which would disrupt our employees'' work, or else at night, which would entail much higher installation charges.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "That option would disrupt work."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The work disrupted is the employees''."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other option is installing at night."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Night installation would cost much more."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since saving money is important, we should have the network installed during the day.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Saving money is important."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That is the reason given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conclusion is to install during the day."}]}], "the_read": "What if the monetary value of lost productivity due to daytime installation is greater than the increased cost of evening installation?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q4", "prompt": "An ingredient in marijuana known as THC has been found to inactivate herpesviruses in experiments. In previous experiments researchers found that inactivated herpesviruses can convert healthy cells into cancer cells. It can be concluded that the use of marijuana can cause cancer.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is an ingredient in marijuana. · The ingredient is called THC. · THC can inactivate herpesviruses. · This was found in experiments. (2) This comes from previous experiments. · Researchers were the ones who found it. · It is about inactivated herpesviruses. · They can convert cells. · The cells converted are healthy ones. · They become cancer cells. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about using marijuana. · Using marijuana can cause cancer.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "An ingredient in marijuana known as THC has been found to inactivate herpesviruses in experiments.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is an ingredient in marijuana."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ingredient is called THC."}, {"n": 3, "text": "THC can inactivate herpesviruses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This was found in experiments."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In previous experiments researchers found that inactivated herpesviruses can convert healthy cells into cancer cells.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This comes from previous experiments."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Researchers were the ones who found it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about inactivated herpesviruses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They can convert cells."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cells converted are healthy ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They become cancer cells."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "It can be concluded that the use of marijuana can cause cancer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about using marijuana."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Using marijuana can cause cancer."}]}], "the_read": "What if other ingredients in marijuana negate THC''s carcinogenic effects?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q5", "prompt": "Archaeologist: A large corporation has recently offered to provide funding to restore an archaeological site and to construct facilities to make the site readily accessible to the general public. The restoration will conform to the best current theories about how the site appeared at the height of the ancient civilization that occupied it. This offer should be rejected, however, because many parts of the site contain unexamined evidence.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An archaeologist is speaking. · A corporation has made an offer. · The corporation is large. · The offer is recent. · The offer is funding. · The funding would restore an archaeological site. · It would also construct facilities. · The facilities would make the site readily accessible to the general public. (2) The restoration will follow theories. · They are the best current theories. · They concern how the site appeared. · The moment described is the height of that civilization. · The civilization was ancient. · That civilization occupied the site. (3) This is the conclusion. · The offer should be rejected. · The reason is unexamined evidence. · Many parts of the site contain it.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Archaeologist: A large corporation has recently offered to provide funding to restore an archaeological site and to construct facilities to make the site readily accessible to the general public.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An archaeologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A corporation has made an offer."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The corporation is large."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The offer is recent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The offer is funding."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The funding would restore an archaeological site."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It would also construct facilities."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The facilities would make the site readily accessible to the general public."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The restoration will conform to the best current theories about how the site appeared at the height of the ancient civilization that occupied it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The restoration will follow theories."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are the best current theories."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They concern how the site appeared."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The moment described is the height of that civilization."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The civilization was ancient."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That civilization occupied the site."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This offer should be rejected, however, because many parts of the site contain unexamined evidence.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The offer should be rejected."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is unexamined evidence."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Many parts of the site contain it."}]}], "the_read": "What if making the site available to the public is more important that preserving unexamined evidence?\nor\nWhat the restoration will not endanger the unexamined evidence?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q6", "prompt": "Besides laying eggs in her own nest, any female wood duck will lay an egg in the nest of another female wood duck if she sees the other duck leaving her nest. Under natural nesting conditions, this parasitic behavior is relatively rare because the ducks'' nests are well hidden. However, when people put up nesting boxes to help the ducks breed, they actually undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts. These nesting boxes become so crowded with extra eggs that few, if any, of the eggs in those boxes hatch.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A female wood duck lays eggs in her own nest. · She will also lay an egg elsewhere. · The other place is another female wood duck''s nest. · This happens under a condition. · The condition is seeing the other duck leave her nest. · This is true of any female wood duck. (2) This is about natural nesting conditions. · The behavior is called parasitic. · It is relatively rare. · The reason is the nests. · The nests are well hidden. (3) This is a contrast. · People put up nesting boxes. · Their intent is to help the ducks breed. · The actual effect is the opposite. · They undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts. (4) The boxes become crowded. · What crowds them is extra eggs. · The crowding has a result. · Few of the eggs hatch. · It is possible that none of them hatch. · This is about eggs in those boxes.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Besides laying eggs in her own nest, any female wood duck will lay an egg in the nest of another female wood duck if she sees the other duck leaving her nest.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A female wood duck lays eggs in her own nest."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She will also lay an egg elsewhere."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other place is another female wood duck''s nest."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This happens under a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is seeing the other duck leave her nest."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is true of any female wood duck."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Under natural nesting conditions, this parasitic behavior is relatively rare because the ducks'' nests are well hidden.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about natural nesting conditions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The behavior is called parasitic."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is relatively rare."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reason is the nests."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The nests are well hidden."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, when people put up nesting boxes to help the ducks breed, they actually undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People put up nesting boxes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their intent is to help the ducks breed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The actual effect is the opposite."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "These nesting boxes become so crowded with extra eggs that few, if any, of the eggs in those boxes hatch.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The boxes become crowded."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What crowds them is extra eggs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The crowding has a result."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Few of the eggs hatch."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is possible that none of them hatch."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is about eggs in those boxes."}]}], "the_read": "What if even fewer duck eggs hatch in the wild due to the wood duck predator''s predilection for omelets?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q7", "prompt": "The crux of creativity resides in the ability to manufacture variations on a theme. If we look at the history of science, for instance, we see that every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas. Careful analysis leads us to understand that what we choose to call a new theme or a new discovery is itself always and without exception some sort of variation, on a deep level, of previous themes.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Creativity has a crux. · The crux is an ability. · The ability is to manufacture variations. · The variations are on a theme. (2) This is an example. · The example is the history of science. · Every idea is built on other ideas. · The number given is a thousand. · Those ideas are related. (3) Careful analysis leads to an understanding. · It is about what we call new. · We call things a new theme or a new discovery. · That is our choice of words. · Such a thing is itself a variation. · This is always true, without exception. · The variation is on a deep level. · It varies previous themes.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The crux of creativity resides in the ability to manufacture variations on a theme.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Creativity has a crux."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The crux is an ability."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ability is to manufacture variations."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The variations are on a theme."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If we look at the history of science, for instance, we see that every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The example is the history of science."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Every idea is built on other ideas."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number given is a thousand."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those ideas are related."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Careful analysis leads us to understand that what we choose to call a new theme or a new discovery is itself always and without exception some sort of variation, on a deep level, of previous themes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Careful analysis leads to an understanding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about what we call new."}, {"n": 3, "text": "We call things a new theme or a new discovery."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is our choice of words."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such a thing is itself a variation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is always true, without exception."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The variation is on a deep level."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It varies previous themes."}]}], "the_read": "What if the \"crux of creativity\" has nothing to do with the history of science? What if these new discoveries or themes are not examples of creativity?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q8", "prompt": "Millions of female bats rear their pups in Bracken Cave. Although the mothers all leave the cave nightly, on their return each mother is almost always swiftly reunited with her own pup. Since the bats'' calls are their only means of finding one another, and a bat pup cannot distinguish the call of its mother from that of any other adult bat, it is clear that each mother bat can recognize the call of her pup.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The 