  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt134-s3',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT134 Section 3',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read it, say it back, react to it. Then the stimulus disappears and you say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Work through the stimulus out loud, a piece at a time. When you have got all of it, press space. The text goes away, and you say the entire argument back from memory.", "rules": ["Everything here is out loud. The whole section records in one take, and it never pauses.", "Read silently, about a sentence at a time. When you are not sure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away and say it back in your own words. Then react to it. Both out loud.", "Keep going like that through the whole stimulus. You pick the pieces. Nothing on screen is counting them.", "Press space when you have got all of it. The stimulus vanishes and stays gone. Now say the whole argument back from memory, with every detail you can hold.", "Press space again and the next one loads. No breaks, no going back, all the way to the end."], "briefing": {"lede": "Worth reading properly once. After that, skim the rules above and go.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still arrive with nothing.", "body": ["You follow a stimulus, sentence by sentence, and it all makes sense as it goes. Then you get down to answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and it is the one this drill was built for.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. If the argument is not still with you while you work the answers, your understanding never got the chance to help.", "Most of what you call reading is built for getting to the end. Scanning names. Dropping the connecting words. Turning a list of three into one label. That is a fine way to read a phone bill. It is a bad way to read something that is about to ask you for every detail back."]}, {"heading": "What you actually do.", "body": ["Three beats through the stimulus, then a fourth once you have got all of it. You decide how much to take on at a time, and nothing on the screen is tracking where you are.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it.", "body": "Intake only. Nothing comes back out yet. Take on about a sentence, with full intonation so every word gets its weight. When you are unsure how much to take, take less. What gets lost when you grab too much is always the front of it."}, {"label": "Say it back.", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, out loud, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it.", "body": "React. A doubt, a question, an example, a joke, a feeling. One or two seconds is plenty. It does not have to be clever, it has to be yours."}, {"label": "The Recall.", "body": "Press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Now say the entire argument back from memory, out loud, with every detail you can still hold. This is the part that gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter.", "body": ["Do not give back the gist, or the summary, or the important parts. You cannot know which parts were important until you have read the whole thing, so filtering on the way through mostly means quietly throwing out the piece you were going to need.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. The names, the numbers, and the small words carrying the load, all, most, only, probably, they either come back or they do not.", "**Efficiency is never a licence to leave information out.**"]}, {"heading": "You will think you have nothing to say. Everybody does.", "body": ["You will open your mouth to colour something and find nothing there. That lasts about a week.", "It is not that you have no thoughts. It is that nobody has ever asked you for one in the middle of reading before, and the habit takes a few sessions to wake up. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count.", "The reaction is what makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away. That part is on you.", "body": ["While you are working the stimulus, nothing on the screen hides. The whole thing stays readable, which means the only thing stopping you rereading a line while you say it back is you, looking away from it.", "That is deliberate. Reading while you recall is a one way street with cars coming both ways. It jams. You stop using your memory at all and slide straight back into the author''s exact words instead of finding your own.", "It is also the version that still works on paper, and on test day. A screen that hides the text for you is a crutch that cannot come with you."]}, {"heading": "Twenty-five minutes, and what that number means.", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section, and it only counts if every stimulus got recalled, coloured, and given a Read. Finishing early with four of them skipped is not finishing early. If you have extra time on test day, your goal scales with it.", "The clock in the corner counts up, not down. It is there so you can pace a section, and it is deliberately not counting towards zero, because a number falling away in the corner of your eye produces exactly the skating read this drill exists to fix.", "It is not a race. It is the pace at which this stops being a study exercise and starts being how you read under a clock. Go slower while it is new. The speed shows up on its own once the method does."]}], "closer": "One a day holds you steady. The second one is where you improve."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, says it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives a Read of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Read against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q1", "prompt": "\"Hot spot\" is a term that ecologists use to describe those habitats with the greatest concentrations of species found only in one place—so-called \"endemic\" species. Many of these hot spots are vulnerable to habitat loss due to commercial development. Furthermore, loss of endemic species accounts for most modern-day extinctions. Thus, given that only a limited number of environmental battles can be waged, it would be reasonable for organizations dedicated to preserving species to _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Hot spot is a term. · Ecologists use that term. · It describes certain habitats. · Those habitats have the greatest concentrations of species. · The species are found only in one place. · Such species are called endemic. (2) This is about the hot spots. · Many of them are vulnerable. · What they are vulnerable to is habitat loss. · The loss is due to commercial development. (3) This is an additional point. · It is about loss of endemic species. · That loss accounts for extinctions. · It accounts for most of them. · The extinctions are modern-day ones. (4) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a given. · The given is that only a limited number of battles can be waged. · The battles are environmental ones. · Something would be reasonable. · It would be reasonable for certain organizations. · Those organizations are dedicated to preserving species.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "\"Hot spot\" is a term that ecologists use to describe those habitats with the greatest concentrations of species found only in one place—so-called \"endemic\" species.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Hot spot is a term."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Ecologists use that term."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It describes certain habitats."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those habitats have the greatest concentrations of species."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The species are found only in one place."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Such species are called endemic."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of these hot spots are vulnerable to habitat loss due to commercial development.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the hot spots."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of them are vulnerable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they are vulnerable to is habitat loss."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The loss is due to commercial development."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Furthermore, loss of endemic species accounts for most modern-day extinctions.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about loss of endemic species."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That loss accounts for extinctions."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It accounts for most of them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The extinctions are modern-day ones."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Thus, given that only a limited number of environmental battles can be waged, it would be reasonable for organizations dedicated to preserving species to _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The given is that only a limited number of battles can be waged."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The battles are environmental ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Something would be reasonable."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would be reasonable for certain organizations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those organizations are dedicated to preserving species."}]}], "the_read": "Protect hot spots.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q2", "prompt": "Principle: If you sell an item that you know to be defective, telling the buyer that the item is sound, you thereby commit fraud.\n\nApplication: Wilton sold a used bicycle to Harris, knowing very little about its condition. Wilton told Harris that the bicycle was in good working condition, but Harris soon learned that the brakes were defective. Wilton was therefore guilty of fraud.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This states a principle. · It is conditional on selling an item. · You know the item to be defective. · You tell the buyer the item is sound. · Doing that commits fraud. (2) This applies the principle. · Wilton sold a bicycle. · The bicycle was used. · He sold it to Harris. · Wilton knew little about its condition. · He knew very little. (3) Wilton told Harris something. · He said the bicycle was in good working condition. · This is a contrast. · Harris found something out. · He learned it soon. · The brakes were defective. (4) This is the conclusion. · Wilton was guilty of something. · What he was guilty of is fraud.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Principle: If you sell an item that you know to be defective, telling the buyer that the item is sound, you thereby commit fraud.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This states a principle."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is conditional on selling an item."}, {"n": 3, "text": "You know the item to be defective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "You tell the buyer the item is sound."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Doing that commits fraud."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Application: Wilton sold a used bicycle to Harris, knowing very little about its condition.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This applies the principle."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wilton sold a bicycle."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bicycle was used."}, {"n": 4, "text": "He sold it to Harris."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Wilton knew little about its condition."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He knew very little."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Wilton told Harris that the bicycle was in good working condition, but Harris soon learned that the brakes were defective.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Wilton told Harris something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He said the bicycle was in good working condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Harris found something out."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He learned it soon."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The brakes were defective."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Wilton was therefore guilty of fraud.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wilton was guilty of something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What he was guilty of is fraud."}]}], "the_read": "What if Wilton didn''t know the bicycle was defective?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q3", "prompt": "Engine noise from boats travelling through killer whales'' habitats ranges in frequency from 100 hertz to 3,000 hertz, an acoustical range that overlaps that in which the whales communicate through screams and squeals. Though killer whales do not seem to behave differently around running boat engines, engine noise from boats can be loud enough to damage their hearing over time. Therefore, _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about engine noise. · The noise comes from boats. · The boats travel through killer whales'' habitats. · The noise ranges in frequency. · The bottom of the range is 100 hertz. · The top of the range is 3,000 hertz. (2) The range is an acoustical one. · It overlaps another range. · The whales communicate in that other range. · They communicate through screams. · They also communicate through squeals. (3) This is a contrast. · Killer whales do not seem to behave differently. · This is around running boat engines. · Engine noise can be loud enough to do damage. · What it damages is their hearing. · The damage happens over time. (4) This is the conclusion. · The word therefore introduces it. · The conclusion itself is left blank.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Engine noise from boats travelling through killer whales'' habitats ranges in frequency from 100 hertz to 3,000 hertz,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about engine noise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The noise comes from boats."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The boats travel through killer whales'' habitats."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The noise ranges in frequency."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The bottom of the range is 100 hertz."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The top of the range is 3,000 hertz."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "an acoustical range that overlaps that in which the whales communicate through screams and squeals.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The range is an acoustical one."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It overlaps another range."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The whales communicate in that other range."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They communicate through screams."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They also communicate through squeals."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Though killer whales do not seem to behave differently around running boat engines, engine noise from boats can be loud enough to damage their hearing over time.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Killer whales do not seem to behave differently."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is around running boat engines."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Engine noise can be loud enough to do damage."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What it damages is their hearing."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The damage happens over time."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The word therefore introduces it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conclusion itself is left blank."}]}], "the_read": "Boat noise may impair whales'' ability to communicate.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q4", "prompt": "Journalist: A manufacturers'' trade group that has long kept its membership list secret inadvertently sent me a document listing hundreds of manufacturing companies. A representative of the trade group later confirmed that every company listed in the document does indeed belong to the trade group. Because Bruch Industries is not listed on the document, it is evidently not a member of the trade group.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A journalist is speaking. · There is a manufacturers'' trade group. · The group keeps its membership list secret. · It has done so for a long time. · The group sent him a document. · The sending was inadvertent. · The document lists manufacturing companies. · It lists hundreds of them. (2) A representative confirmed something. · The representative is of the trade group. · The confirmation came later. · Every company listed does belong to the group. · The companies meant are those in the document. (3) This is the reason given. · Bruch Industries is not listed on the document. · This is the conclusion. · Bruch Industries is not a member of the group. · This is marked as evident.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Journalist: A manufacturers'' trade group that has long kept its membership list secret inadvertently sent me a document listing hundreds of manufacturing companies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A journalist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a manufacturers'' trade group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The group keeps its membership list secret."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It has done so for a long time."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The group sent him a document."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The sending was inadvertent."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The document lists manufacturing companies."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It lists hundreds of them."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A representative of the trade group later confirmed that every company listed in the document does indeed belong to the trade group.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A representative confirmed something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The representative is of the trade group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The confirmation came later."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Every company listed does belong to the group."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The companies meant are those in the document."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Because Bruch Industries is not listed on the document, it is evidently not a member of the trade group.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Bruch Industries is not listed on the document."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Bruch Industries is not a member of the group."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}], "the_read": "What if the document doesn''t list every member of the trade group?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q5", "prompt": "Peter: Unlike in the past, most children''s stories nowadays don''t have clearly immoral characters in them. They should, though. Children need to learn the consequences of being bad.\n\nYoko: Children''s stories still tend to have clearly immoral characters in them, but now these characters tend not to be the sort that frighten children. Surely that''s an improvement.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Peter is the first speaker. · This is a contrast with the past. · It is about children''s stories nowadays. · Most of them lack immoral characters. · Those characters would be clearly immoral. (2) This is a contrast. · He says they should. · What they should have is such characters. (3) This is his reason. · Children need to learn something. · What they learn is consequences. · The consequences are of being bad. (4) Yoko is the second speaker. · Children''s stories still tend to have such characters. · The characters are clearly immoral. · This is a contrast. · The characters now tend to be different. · They are not the sort that frighten children. (5) This is her conclusion. · The change is an improvement. · She treats this as sure.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter: Unlike in the past, most children''s stories nowadays don''t have clearly immoral characters in them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with the past."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about children''s stories nowadays."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Most of them lack immoral characters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those characters would be clearly immoral."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "They should, though.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He says they should."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they should have is such characters."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Children need to learn the consequences of being bad.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Children need to learn something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they learn is consequences."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The consequences are of being bad."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Yoko: Children''s stories still tend to have clearly immoral characters in them, but now these characters tend not to be the sort that frighten children.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yoko is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Children''s stories still tend to have such characters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The characters are clearly immoral."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The characters now tend to be different."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They are not the sort that frighten children."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Surely that''s an improvement.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is her conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The change is an improvement."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She treats this as sure."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether children''s stories today tend to have clearly immoral characters.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q6", "prompt": "Local resident: An overabundance of algae must be harmful to the smaller fish in this pond. During the fifteen or so years that I have lived here, the few times that I have seen large numbers of dead small fish wash ashore in late summer coincide exactly with the times that I have noticed abnormally large amounts of algae in the water.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A local resident is speaking. · This is the conclusion. · It is about an overabundance of algae. · The algae must be harmful. · What it harms is the smaller fish. · The fish are in this pond. (2) This is about a period of years. · The number is about fifteen. · He has lived here that long. (3) He has seen dead small fish wash ashore. · The numbers were large. · He saw this only a few times. · It happened in late summer. · Those times coincide with other times. · The coincidence is exact. · The other times are when he noticed large amounts of algae. · The amounts were abnormally large.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Local resident: An overabundance of algae must be harmful to the smaller fish in this pond.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A local resident is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about an overabundance of algae."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The algae must be harmful."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What it harms is the smaller fish."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The fish are in this pond."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "During the fifteen or so years that I have lived here,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a period of years."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The number is about fifteen."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He has lived here that long."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "the few times that I have seen large numbers of dead small fish wash ashore in late summer coincide exactly with the times that I have noticed abnormally large amounts of algae in the water.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "He has seen dead small fish wash ashore."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The numbers were large."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He saw this only a few times."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It happened in late summer."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those times coincide with other times."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The coincidence is exact."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The other times are when he noticed large amounts of algae."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The amounts were abnormally large."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between the algae and the dead fish? What if pollution is causing both the algae growth and the fish deaths? What if dead fish cause algae to grow?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q7", "prompt": "Tanner: The public should demand political debates before any election. Voters are better able to choose the candidate best suited for office if they watch the candidates seriously debate one another.\n\nSaldana: Political debates almost always benefit the candidate who has the better debating skills. Thus, they don''t really help voters determine which candidate is most qualified for office.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Tanner is the first speaker. · This is his recommendation. · The public should demand political debates. · The debates would come before an election. · This applies to any election. (2) This is his reason. · Voters would be better able to choose. · They would choose the candidate best suited for office. · This holds if they watch the candidates debate. · The debating is serious. · The candidates debate one another. (3) Saldana is the second speaker. · Political debates benefit one candidate. · That candidate has the better debating skills. · This is almost always so. (4) This is her conclusion. · Debates do not help voters. · What voters would determine is which candidate is most qualified. · The qualification is for office.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tanner: The public should demand political debates before any election.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tanner is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is his recommendation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The public should demand political debates."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The debates would come before an election."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This applies to any election."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Voters are better able to choose the candidate best suited for office if they watch the candidates seriously debate one another.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Voters would be better able to choose."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They would choose the candidate best suited for office."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds if they watch the candidates debate."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The debating is serious."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The candidates debate one another."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Saldana: Political debates almost always benefit the candidate who has the better debating skills.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Saldana is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Political debates benefit one candidate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That candidate has the better debating skills."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is almost always so."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Thus, they don''t really help voters determine which candidate is most qualified for office.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is her conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Debates do not help voters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What voters would determine is which candidate is most qualified."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The qualification is for office."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether political debates help voters decide who is more qualified for office.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q8", "prompt": "A recent study shows that those highways that carry the most traffic, and thus tend to be the most congested, have the lowest rate of fatal traffic accidents.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a study. · The study is recent. · It is about certain highways. · Those highways carry the most traffic. · They therefore tend to be the most congested. · Those highways have the lowest rate of accidents. · The accidents are fatal traffic accidents.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A recent study shows that those highways that carry the most traffic, and thus tend to be the most congested, have the lowest rate of fatal traffic accidents.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study is recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about certain highways."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those highways carry the most traffic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They therefore tend to be the most congested."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those highways have the lowest rate of accidents."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The accidents are fatal traffic accidents."}]}], "the_read": "What if people can''t drive particularly fast on these roads because of the heavy traffic, and fatal accidents tend to occur at high speeds?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q9", "prompt": "In some jurisdictions, lawmakers have instituted sentencing guidelines that mandate a penalty for theft that is identical to the one they have mandated for bribery. Hence, lawmakers in those jurisdictions evidently consider the harm resulting from theft to be equal to the harm resulting from bribery.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This holds in some jurisdictions. · Lawmakers instituted sentencing guidelines. · The guidelines mandate a penalty for theft. · That penalty is identical to another. · The comparison is with the penalty mandated for bribery. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about lawmakers in those jurisdictions. · They consider two harms equal. · One is the harm resulting from theft. · The other is the harm resulting from bribery. · This is marked as evident.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In some jurisdictions, lawmakers have instituted sentencing guidelines that mandate a penalty for theft that is identical to the one they have mandated for bribery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This holds in some jurisdictions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Lawmakers instituted sentencing guidelines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The guidelines mandate a penalty for theft."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That penalty is identical to another."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the penalty mandated for bribery."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Hence, lawmakers in those jurisdictions evidently consider the harm resulting from theft to be equal to the harm resulting from bribery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about lawmakers in those jurisdictions."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They consider two harms equal."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One is the harm resulting from theft."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other is the harm resulting from bribery."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}], "the_read": "What if penalties for a crime have no relationship to the harm lawmakers think those crimes impose?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q10", "prompt": "People often admonish us to learn the lessons of history, but, even if it were easy to discover what the past was really like, it is nearly impossible to discover its lessons. We are supposed, for example, to learn the lessons of World War I. But what are they? And were we ever to discover what they are, it is not clear that we could ever apply them, for we shall never again have a situation just like World War I.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) People admonish us to learn history''s lessons. · They do this often. · This is a contrast. · Discovering what the past was like might be easy. · Discovering its lessons is nearly impossible. (2) This is an example. · We are supposed to learn some lessons. · Those lessons are World War I''s. (3) This is a contrast. · He asks a question. · The question is what those lessons are. (4) This is conditional on discovering them. · It is not clear we could apply them. · We could never apply them. · This is the reason given. · We shall never again have such a situation. · The situation would be just like World War I.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "People often admonish us to learn the lessons of history, but, even if it were easy to discover what the past was really like, it is nearly impossible to discover its lessons.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "People admonish us to learn history''s lessons."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They do this often."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Discovering what the past was like might be easy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Discovering its lessons is nearly impossible."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "We are supposed, for example, to learn the lessons of World War I.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "We are supposed to learn some lessons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those lessons are World War I''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But what are they?", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He asks a question."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The question is what those lessons are."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "And were we ever to discover what they are, it is not clear that we could ever apply them, for we shall never again have a situation just like World War I.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on discovering them."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is not clear we could apply them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "We could never apply them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 5, "text": "We shall never again have such a situation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The situation would be just like World War I."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can discover the lessons of history without knowing exactly what they are or applying them in identical situations?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q11", "prompt": "Sigerson argues that the city should adopt ethical guidelines that preclude its politicians from accepting campaign contributions from companies that do business with the city. Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest, however, because he has taken contributions from such companies throughout his career in city politics.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Sigerson makes an argument. · He says the city should adopt ethical guidelines. · The guidelines would preclude something. · What is precluded is politicians accepting campaign contributions. · The politicians are the city''s. · The contributions come from certain companies. · Those companies do business with the city. (2) This is the conclusion. · Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest. · This is a contrast. · This is the reason given. · He has taken contributions from such companies. · He did so throughout his career. · The career is in city politics.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sigerson argues that the city should adopt ethical guidelines that preclude its politicians from accepting campaign contributions from companies that do business with the city.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sigerson makes an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He says the city should adopt ethical guidelines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The guidelines would preclude something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is precluded is politicians accepting campaign contributions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The politicians are the city''s."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The contributions come from certain companies."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those companies do business with the city."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest, however, because he has taken contributions from such companies throughout his career in city politics.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He has taken contributions from such companies."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He did so throughout his career."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The career is in city politics."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because Sigerson has acted in a way that is counter to his proposal in the past, that doesn''t mean his proposal is dishonest?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q12", "prompt": "Some gardening books published by Garden Path Press recommend tilling the soil and adding compost before starting a new garden on a site, but they do not explain the difference between hot and cold composting. Since any gardening book that recommends adding compost is flawed if it does not explain at least the basics of composting, some books published by Garden Path are flawed.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about some 