is that government can be trusted."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would be trusted to act in the public''s interest."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because the argument relies on a questionable assumption, that doesn''t mean it''s totally wrong?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q15", "prompt": "Columnist: Shortsighted motorists learn the hard way about the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance; such maintenance almost always pays off in the long run. Our usually shortsighted city council should be praised for using similar wisdom when they hired a long-term economic development adviser. In hiring this adviser, the council made an investment that is likely to have a big payoff in several years. Other cities in this region that have devoted resources to economic development planning have earned large returns on such an investment.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A columnist is speaking. · Some motorists are shortsighted. · They learn the hard way. · What they learn is the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance. · Such maintenance pays off. · It pays off in the long run. · It does so almost always. (2) This is about the city council. · The council is usually shortsighted. · The council should be praised. · It used similar wisdom. · It hired an adviser. · The adviser is for long-term economic development. (3) Hiring the adviser was an investment. · The council made it. · The investment is likely to have a payoff. · The payoff would be big. · It would come in several years. (4) This is about other cities. · Those cities are in this region. · They devoted resources to economic development planning. · Those cities earned returns. · The returns were large. · The returns came on such an investment.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Columnist: Shortsighted motorists learn the hard way about the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance; such maintenance almost always pays off in the long run.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A columnist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some motorists are shortsighted."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They learn the hard way."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they learn is the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such maintenance pays off."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It pays off in the long run."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It does so almost always."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Our usually shortsighted city council should be praised for using similar wisdom when they hired a long-term economic development adviser.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the city council."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The council is usually shortsighted."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The council should be praised."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It used similar wisdom."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It hired an adviser."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The adviser is for long-term economic development."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In hiring this adviser, the council made an investment that is likely to have a big payoff in several years.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Hiring the adviser was an investment."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The council made it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The investment is likely to have a payoff."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The payoff would be big."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would come in several years."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Other cities in this region that have devoted resources to economic development planning have earned large returns on such an investment.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about other cities."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those cities are in this region."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They devoted resources to economic development planning."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cities earned returns."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The returns were large."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The returns came on such an investment."}]}], "the_read": "What if this city is not comparable to other cities that have seen large returns on the investment in an economic development adviser, so this city won''t see the same returns?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q16", "prompt": "Editorial: Cell-phone usage on buses and trains is annoying to other passengers. This suggests that recent proposals to allow use of cell phones on airplanes are ill-advised. Cell-phone use would be far more upsetting on airplanes than it is on buses and trains. Airline passengers are usually packed in tightly. And if airline passengers are offended by the cell-phone excesses of their seatmates, they often cannot move to another seat.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is an editorial. · It is about cell-phone usage. · The usage is on buses. · It is also on trains. · The usage annoys other passengers. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is suggested rather than proven. · There are certain proposals. · The proposals are recent. · They would allow cell phone use on airplanes. · The proposals are ill-advised. (3) Cell-phone use would be upsetting on airplanes. · It would be far more upsetting. · The comparison is with buses and trains. (4) This is about airline passengers. · They are packed in tightly. · This is usually so. (5) This is an additional point. · It is conditional on passengers being offended. · What offends them is their seatmates'' cell-phone excesses. · They cannot move to another seat. · This is often so.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Editorial: Cell-phone usage on buses and trains is annoying to other passengers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about cell-phone usage."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The usage is on buses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is also on trains."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The usage annoys other passengers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This suggests that recent proposals to allow use of cell phones on airplanes are ill-advised.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is suggested rather than proven."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There are certain proposals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The proposals are recent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They would allow cell phone use on airplanes."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The proposals are ill-advised."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Cell-phone use would be far more upsetting on airplanes than it is on buses and trains.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Cell-phone use would be upsetting on airplanes."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It would be far more upsetting."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with buses and trains."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Airline passengers are usually packed in tightly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about airline passengers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are packed in tightly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is usually so."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "And if airline passengers are offended by the cell-phone excesses of their seatmates, they often cannot move to another seat.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is conditional on passengers being offended."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What offends them is their seatmates'' cell-phone excesses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They cannot move to another seat."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is often so."}]}], "the_read": "What if cell phone usage on buses and trains are annoying because people are making phone calls, but people would only be allowed to use a phone to text on airplanes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q17", "prompt": "Science writer: The deterioration of cognitive faculties associated with Alzheimer''s disease is evidently caused by the activities of microglia—the brain''s own immune cells. For one thing, this deterioration can be slowed by some anti-inflammatory drugs, such as acetylsalicylic acid. Furthermore, patients with Alzheimer''s are unable to eliminate the protein BA from the brain, where it accumulates and forms deposits. The microglia attack these protein deposits by releasing poisons that destroy surrounding healthy brain cells, thereby impairing the brain''s cognitive functions.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A science writer is speaking. · This is the conclusion. · It is about deterioration of cognitive faculties. · That deterioration is associated with Alzheimer''s disease. · It is caused by the activities of microglia. · Microglia are the brain''s own immune cells. · This is marked as evident. (2) This is the first reason given. · The deterioration can be slowed. · Some anti-inflammatory drugs slow it. · Acetylsalicylic acid is given as an example. (3) This is an additional reason. · It is about patients with Alzheimer''s. · They cannot eliminate a protein. · The protein is BA. · They cannot eliminate it from the brain. · The protein accumulates there. · The protein forms deposits. (4) The microglia attack the protein deposits. · They attack by releasing poisons. · The poisons destroy brain cells. · Those cells are healthy ones. · The cells are the surrounding ones. · Doing so impairs the brain''s cognitive functions.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Science writer: The deterioration of cognitive faculties associated with Alzheimer''s disease is evidently caused by the activities of microglia—the brain''s own immune cells.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A science writer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about deterioration of cognitive faculties."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That deterioration is associated with Alzheimer''s disease."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is caused by the activities of microglia."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Microglia are the brain''s own immune cells."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "For one thing, this deterioration can be slowed by some anti-inflammatory drugs, such as acetylsalicylic acid.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the first reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The deterioration can be slowed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some anti-inflammatory drugs slow it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Acetylsalicylic acid is given as an example."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Furthermore, patients with Alzheimer''s are unable to eliminate the protein BA from the brain, where it accumulates and forms deposits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about patients with Alzheimer''s."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They cannot eliminate a protein."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The protein is BA."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They cannot eliminate it from the brain."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The protein accumulates there."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The protein forms deposits."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The microglia attack these protein deposits by releasing poisons that destroy surrounding healthy brain cells, thereby impairing the brain''s cognitive functions.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The microglia attack the protein deposits."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They attack by releasing poisons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The poisons destroy brain cells."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cells are healthy ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cells are the surrounding ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Doing so impairs the brain''s cognitive functions."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no relationship between microglia and Alzheimer''s? What if something else causes Alzheimer''s? What if an Alzheimer''s diagnosis increases the amount of microglia in the brain?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q18", "prompt": "Lawyer: One is justified in accessing information in computer files without securing authorization from the computer''s owner only if the computer is typically used in the operation of a business. If, in addition, there exist reasonable grounds for believing that such a computer contains data usable as evidence in a legal proceeding against the computer''s owner, then accessing the data in those computer files without the owner''s authorization is justified.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A lawyer is speaking. · This states a necessary condition. · It is about accessing information in computer files. · The access is without the owner''s authorization. · Justification requires one thing. · The computer must be typically used in the operation of a business. (2) This adds a further condition. · There must exist grounds for a belief. · The grounds must be reasonable. · The belief is that the computer contains data. · The data would be usable as evidence. · The evidence would be in a legal proceeding. · The proceeding would be against the computer''s owner. (3) This is the consequent. · Accessing the data is justified. · The data is in those computer files. · The access is without the owner''s authorization.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Lawyer: One is justified in accessing information in computer files without securing authorization from the computer''s owner only if the computer is typically used in the operation of a business.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A lawyer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This states a necessary condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about accessing information in computer files."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The access is without the owner''s authorization."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Justification requires one thing."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The computer must be typically used in the operation of a business."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If, in addition, there exist reasonable grounds for believing that such a computer contains data usable as evidence in a legal proceeding against the computer''s owner,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a further condition."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There must exist grounds for a belief."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The grounds must be reasonable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The belief is that the computer contains data."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The data would be usable as evidence."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The evidence would be in a legal proceeding."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The proceeding would be against the computer''s owner."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "then accessing the data in those computer files without the owner''s authorization is justified.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the consequent."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Accessing the data is justified."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The data is in those computer files."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The access is without the owner''s authorization."}]}], "the_read": "If a computer is not typically used in the operation of a business, then one is not justified in accessing information in the files without the owner''s permission.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q19", "prompt": "The conventional process for tanning leather uses large amounts of calcium oxide and sodium sulfide. Tanning leather using biological catalysts costs about the same as using these conventional chemicals if the cost of waste disposal is left out of the comparison. However, nearly 20 percent less waste is produced with biological catalysts, and waste disposal is a substantial part of the overall cost of tanning. It is therefore less costly to tan leather if biological catalysts are used instead.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a conventional process. · The process is for tanning leather. · It uses calcium oxide. · It also uses sodium sulfide. · The amounts are large. (2) Leather can be tanned using biological catalysts. · That costs about the same. · The comparison is with using the conventional chemicals. · This holds under a condition. · The condition is leaving out the cost of waste disposal. (3) This is a contrast. · Less waste is produced with biological catalysts. · The figure is nearly 20 percent less. · Waste disposal is part of the overall cost. · That part is substantial. · The cost is the overall cost of tanning. (4) This is the conclusion. · Tanning leather is less costly. · This holds if biological catalysts are used. · They would be used instead of the chemicals.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The conventional process for tanning leather uses large amounts of calcium oxide and sodium sulfide.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a conventional process."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The process is for tanning leather."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It uses calcium oxide."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It also uses sodium sulfide."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The amounts are large."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Tanning leather using biological catalysts costs about the same as using these conventional chemicals if the cost of waste disposal is left out of the comparison.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Leather can be tanned using biological catalysts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That costs about the same."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with using the conventional chemicals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds under a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is leaving out the cost of waste disposal."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, nearly 20 percent less waste is produced with biological catalysts, and waste disposal is a substantial part of the overall cost of tanning.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Less waste is produced with biological catalysts."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The figure is nearly 20 percent less."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Waste disposal is part of the overall cost."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That part is substantial."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The cost is the overall cost of tanning."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is therefore less costly to tan leather if biological catalysts are used instead.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Tanning leather is less costly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This holds if biological catalysts are used."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They would be used instead of the chemicals."}]}], "the_read": "What if it costs much more to dispose of biological catalysts than to dispose of chemicals?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q20", "prompt": "One should not play a practical joke on someone if it shows contempt for that person or if one believes it might bring significant harm to that person.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) One should not play a practical joke. · The joke would be on someone. · Two conditions are given. · One condition is that it shows contempt. · The contempt would be for that person. · The other condition is a belief. · The belief is that it might bring harm. · The harm would be significant.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "One should not play a practical joke on someone if it shows contempt for that person or if one believes it might bring significant harm to that person.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "One should not play a practical joke."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The joke would be on someone."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Two conditions are given."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One condition is that it shows contempt."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The contempt would be for that person."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The other condition is a belief."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The belief is that it might bring harm."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The harm would be significant."}]}], "the_read": "If one should play a practical joke on another person, then the joke does not show contempt and the one playing the joke does not believe it might bring significant harm.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q21", "prompt": "Economics professor: Marty''s Pizza and Checkers Pizza are the two major pizza parlors in our town. Marty''s sold coupon books including coupons good for one large plain pizza at any local pizza parlor, at Marty''s expense. But Checkers refused to accept these coupons, even though they were redeemed by all other local pizza parlors. Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing and would have satisfied those of its potential customers who had purchased the coupon books. This shows that Checkers''s motive in refusing to accept the coupons was simply to hurt Marty''s Pizza.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An economics professor is speaking. · There are two major pizza parlors. · One is Marty''s Pizza. · The other is Checkers Pizza. · They are in his town. (2) Marty''s sold coupon books. · The books included coupons. · Each coupon was good for one large plain pizza. · The coupon was good at any local pizza parlor. · The cost fell on Marty''s. (3) This is a contrast. · Checkers refused to accept the coupons. · This is despite something. · All other local pizza parlors redeemed them. (4) Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing. · It would also have satisfied some people. · Those people are its potential customers. · Those customers had purchased the coupon books. (5) This is the conclusion. · It is about Checkers''s motive. · The motive was in refusing the coupons. 