e motive was in refusing the coupons."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The motive was to hurt Marty''s Pizza."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That was the only motive."}]}], "the_read": "What if Checkers''s has a company policy to not accept coupons not issued by them and therefore the motive is to follow policy? What if it would have been super inconvenient and an administrative hassle for Checkers to accept the coupon?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q22", "prompt": "Science writer: Scientists'' astounding success rate with research problems they have been called upon to solve causes the public to believe falsely that science can solve any problem. In fact, the problems scientists are called upon to solve are typically selected by scientists themselves. When the problems are instead selected by politicians or business leaders, their formulation is nevertheless guided by scientists in such a way as to make scientific solutions feasible. Scientists are almost never asked to solve problems that are not subject to such formulation.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A science writer is speaking. · Scientists have a success rate. · The rate is astounding. · It is with problems they were called upon to solve. · That success causes a public belief. · The belief is that science can solve any problem. · The belief is false. (2) This is the correction. · It is about the problems scientists are called upon to solve. · Scientists themselves select them. · This is typically so. (3) This is the other case. · The problems are selected by someone else. · Politicians may select them. · Business leaders may select them. (4) This is a contrast. · The problems still get formulated. · Scientists guide the formulation. · The guidance has a purpose. · The purpose is to make scientific solutions feasible. (5) Scientists are almost never asked something. · What they are not asked is to solve certain problems. · Those problems are not subject to such formulation.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Science writer: Scientists'' astounding success rate with research problems they have been called upon to solve causes the public to believe falsely that science can solve any problem.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A science writer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Scientists have a success rate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rate is astounding."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is with problems they were called upon to solve."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That success causes a public belief."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The belief is that science can solve any problem."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The belief is false."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In fact, the problems scientists are called upon to solve are typically selected by scientists themselves.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the correction."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the problems scientists are called upon to solve."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Scientists themselves select them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is typically so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "When the problems are instead selected by politicians or business leaders,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the other case."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The problems are selected by someone else."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Politicians may select them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Business leaders may select them."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "their formulation is nevertheless guided by scientists in such a way as to make scientific solutions feasible.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The problems still get formulated."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Scientists guide the formulation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The guidance has a purpose."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The purpose is to make scientific solutions feasible."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Scientists are almost never asked to solve problems that are not subject to such formulation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Scientists are almost never asked something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What they are not asked is to solve certain problems."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those problems are not subject to such formulation."}]}], "the_read": "Scientists may not have the same problem solving success rate if they did not choose or help to formulate the problems they are called upon to solve.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q23", "prompt": "Most auto mechanics have extensive experience. Furthermore, most mechanics with extensive experience understand electronic circuits. Thus, most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about auto mechanics. · Most of them have experience. · The experience is extensive. (2) This is an additional premise. · It is about mechanics with extensive experience. · Most of them understand electronic circuits. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about auto mechanics. · Most of them understand electronic circuits.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Most auto mechanics have extensive experience.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about auto mechanics."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most of them have experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The experience is extensive."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Furthermore, most mechanics with extensive experience understand electronic circuits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional premise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about mechanics with extensive experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most of them understand electronic circuits."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus, most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about auto mechanics."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most of them understand electronic circuits."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can''t use bad some/most conditional reasoning?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q24", "prompt": "If one wants to succeed, then one should act as though one were genuinely confident about one''s abilities, even if one actually distrusts one''s skills. Success is much more easily obtained by those who genuinely believe themselves capable of succeeding than by those filled with self-doubts.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is conditional on wanting to succeed. · One should act a certain way. · One should act as though genuinely confident. · The confidence would be about one''s abilities. · This holds even in one case. · That case is actually distrusting one''s skills. (2) This is the reason given. · Success is more easily obtained by some people. · It is much more easily obtained. · Those people genuinely believe themselves capable. · The comparison is with those filled with self-doubts.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "If one wants to succeed, then one should act as though one were genuinely confident about one''s abilities, even if one actually distrusts one''s skills.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on wanting to succeed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One should act a certain way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One should act as though genuinely confident."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The confidence would be about one''s abilities."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds even in one case."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That case is actually distrusting one''s skills."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Success is much more easily obtained by those who genuinely believe themselves capable of succeeding than by those filled with self-doubts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Success is more easily obtained by some people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is much more easily obtained."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people genuinely believe themselves capable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with those filled with self-doubts."}]}], "the_read": "What if genuine self confidence cannot be gained from fake-it-til-you-make-it?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 25, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q25", "prompt": "Journalist: The trade union members at AutoFaber Inc. are planning to go on strike. Independent arbitration would avert a strike, but only if both sides agree to accept the arbitrator''s recommendations as binding. However, based on past experience, the union is quite unlikely to agree to this, so a strike is likely.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A journalist is speaking. · Trade union members are planning something. · The members are at AutoFaber Inc. · They plan to go on strike. (2) Independent arbitration would avert a strike. · This holds under one condition only. · Both sides must agree to something. · They must accept the arbitrator''s recommendations. · They must accept them as binding. (3) This is a contrast. · The claim is based on past experience. · The union is unlikely to agree. · It is quite unlikely. · This is the conclusion. · A strike is likely.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Journalist: The trade union members at AutoFaber Inc. are planning to go on strike.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A journalist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Trade union members are planning something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The members are at AutoFaber Inc."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They plan to go on strike."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Independent arbitration would avert a strike, but only if both sides agree to accept the arbitrator''s recommendations as binding.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Independent arbitration would avert a strike."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds under one condition only."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Both sides must agree to something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They must accept the arbitrator''s recommendations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They must accept them as binding."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, based on past experience, the union is quite unlikely to agree to this, so a strike is likely.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claim is based on past experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The union is unlikely to agree."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is quite unlikely."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A strike is likely."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because arbitration was unlikely in the past, that doesn''t mean it''s unlikely now?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 26, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q26", "prompt": "Acquiring complete detailed information about all the pros and cons of a product one might purchase would clearly be difficult and expensive. It is rational not to acquire such information unless one expects that the benefits of doing so will outweigh the cost and difficulty of doing so. Therefore, consumers who do not bother to acquire such information are thereby behaving rationally.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about acquiring information. · The information would be complete and detailed. · It concerns all the pros and cons. · They are of a product one might purchase. · Acquiring it would be difficult. · It would also be expensive. · This is treated as clear. (2) Not acquiring such information is rational. · This holds unless one expects something. · The expectation is that benefits will outweigh. · The benefits are of acquiring it. · What they outweigh is the cost and difficulty. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about certain consumers. · Those consumers do not bother to acquire the information. · They are behaving rationally.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Acquiring complete detailed information about all the pros and cons of a product one might purchase would clearly be difficult and expensive.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about acquiring information."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The information would be complete and detailed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It concerns all the pros and cons."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are of a product one might purchase."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Acquiring it would be difficult."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would also be expensive."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is treated as clear."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is rational not to acquire such information unless one expects that the benefits of doing so will outweigh the cost and difficulty of doing so.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Not acquiring such information is rational."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds unless one expects something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The expectation is that benefits will outweigh."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The benefits are of acquiring it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What they outweigh is the cost and difficulty."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, consumers who do not bother to acquire such information are thereby behaving rationally.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about certain consumers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those consumers do not bother to acquire the information."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are behaving rationally."}]}], "the_read": "What if the benefits of acquiring the information are always expected to outweigh the cost?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q26",